Method: cp -a scratch copies (cb9_before = HEAD b1f0f36 via git stash/pop,
cb9_after = CB9 working-tree diff applied), throwaway httptest probes
against a copy of real ./data (never the live binary/port). Compared with
python3 (see note on `diff` below).

1. REAL DATA, BYTE-IDENTICAL — PASS.
   Probe hit all 6 export kinds, month-detail JSON for 2023-01..2026-12,
   KPI-detail HTML, and the budget-vs-actual chart endpoint on both
   before/after builds over the full default range (MinDate/MaxDate).
   154 files compared byte-for-byte (python3, not `diff` — `diff -rq`
   falsely reported "Files are identical" for files that provably differ,
   consistent with the known rtk-diff-falsification hazard; all real
   comparisons here used a raw-bytes python3 script). Result: 102 files
   differ, ALL with Count==0 (no real transactions), ALL a pure "-0"→"0"
   token change (confirmed by regex+JSON diff); every export/detail/chart
   file for a real populated month is byte-identical. Exports and
   month-detail HTML never hit the empty-month path with default full
   range (classifiedMonthlyTotals/GroupByMonth skip zero-transaction
   months entirely), so the CB7.3 claim of a live "-0.00" export cell
   was NOT actually observed on real data with the default range; it IS
   reproducible with an explicit out-of-range custom start/end
   (month-detail JSON for e.g. 2023-01 read -0 before the fix, +0 after —
   confirmed via `/dashboard/kpi/healthcare/month/2023-01`).

2. REMAINING -0 SOURCES — no in-scope leak, one out-of-scope leak found.
   `grep -rn '= -[a-zA-Z_]*[.(]\|:= -[a-zA-Z_]*[.(]'` outside
   metrics.go/dashboard/explorer: all are `total += -t.Amount` /
   `agg[k] -= t.Amount` accumulator loops (majorexpenses chart bucketing,
   transfers, whatif/sync, curate/expenses). Verified in Go: an
   accumulator starting at literal `0.0` can never land on IEEE -0.0
   under exact cancellation (a + (-a) = +0 in round-to-nearest, always) —
   confirmed empirically. These are genuinely safe; manifest's exclusion
   is correct.
   FOUND (out of CB9's written scope, reported as observation not FAIL):
   `internal/services/insights/trends.go` dailyAvg/historicalDaily/
   spentSoFar are direct `-X.SumAmount()/days` divisions (not
   accumulators) feeding `insights.SpendingVelocity`, which is consumed
   by BOTH the insights dashboard AND the MCP `get_trends` velocity row
   via `internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/trends.go:velocityRowFrom` ->
   `round2()`. Confirmed `round2(math.Copysign(0,-1))` = -0 and
   `json.Marshal` emits the literal token `-0` (Go). This is a genuine
   MCP-tool -0 leak reachable whenever a period's outflows sum to exactly
   zero, but it lives in a different package/file than anything CB9's
   manifest claims to touch (CB9's mcpsvc/spend/trends.go change is a
   doc-comment only) and predates CB9 (already present at accepted HEAD
   b1f0f36). Backlog item, not a CB9 defect.

3. FORMATTERS — formatPercent belt verified correct
   (math.Copysign(0,-1) -> "v=0" clears the sign bit, confirmed in a
   throwaway Go program; mutant-killed, see #4). `formatWholeDollars` and
   `formatAbbreviatedTotal` are immune (route through int64, which has no
   signed zero). `formatNumber` (%.0f) has the IDENTICAL latent bug
   (confirmed `%.0f` of -0.0 prints "-0", and formatNumber's `v<0` guard
   does not catch it) but its only callers are whatif/retirement
   templates, never fed by a negated transaction sum — out of scope,
   flagged as observation. JS: `web/static/js/charts.js` only formatter
   observed is `.toFixed()` (verified safe for -0 in Node) plus
   `Intl.NumberFormat.format()` (confirmed leaks "-$0.00" for JS -0), but
   its only callers (`merchantTotals[...] -= t.Amount`,
   majorExpense-bucket `total += -t.Amount`) are accumulator patterns,
   proven -0-safe per #2. No live JS leak.

4. TEST STRENGTH — FAIL GROUND.
